Here's the complete migration to ASP.NET Core (.NET 8):

## Pattern Inventory

Your code has two `Thread.Abort`-related patterns:

| Location | Pattern | API |
|----------|---------|-----|
| `btnExport_Click` | ASP.NET request termination | `Response.End()` |
| `btnRedirect_Click` | ASP.NET request termination + control flow | `Response.Redirect(url, true)` |
| `btnRedirect_Click` | `ThreadAbortException` as control flow | `catch (ThreadAbortException)` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // Response.End() → return a FileContentResult.
    // No need to Clear/AddHeader/Write/End — the framework handles the
    // full response lifecycle when you return an IActionResult.
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();
        var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
        return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
    }

    // Response.Redirect(url, true) → return RedirectResult.
    // No ThreadAbortException exists in .NET 8 — use normal control flow.
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

**`Response.End()`** — Removed entirely. In .NET Framework, `Response.End()` calls `Thread.Abort` internally to halt execution. In ASP.NET Core, you simply return an `IActionResult`. The framework writes the response and ends the pipeline — no abort needed.

**`Response.Redirect(url, true)`** — The `true` (endResponse) parameter triggered `Thread.Abort` to stop further code execution after the redirect. In ASP.NET Core, returning `Redirect()` from the action is a normal return statement — execution naturally stops.

**`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** — Deleted. This catch block only existed because `Response.Redirect(url, true)` threw `ThreadAbortException` as a side effect. With `return Redirect(...)`, normal control flow makes this unnecessary.

**Null-conditional on `User.Identity`** — `User.Identity` can be `null` in ASP.NET Core when no authentication middleware has run. The `?.` avoids a `NullReferenceException`.

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Writing to `Response` then returning an `IActionResult`** | Don't mix `Response.Body.WriteAsync()` with returning `File()` or `Redirect()`. Pick one approach — return an `IActionResult` in controller actions. |
| **Assuming `ThreadAbortException` still exists** | It compiles (the type exists in the BCL) but `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. Any `catch (ThreadAbortException)` is dead code. |
| **Using `Response.Redirect()` on `HttpResponse` instead of the `Controller.Redirect()` helper** | `HttpResponse.Redirect()` doesn't stop execution — code after it still runs. Always `return Redirect(...)` to exit the action. |
| **Forgetting `[HttpPost]`** | The original code was bound to button click handlers (postbacks). Map these to `[HttpPost]` endpoints with corresponding form actions or AJAX calls. |
| **Not handling `CancellationToken` for long reports** | If `GenerateReport()` is slow, accept `CancellationToken` to honor client disconnects: |

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core provides HttpContext.RequestAborted via this parameter.
    // If the client disconnects, the token is cancelled automatically.
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data);
    return File(bytes, "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

This replaces the "abort the thread on disconnect" behavior that .NET Framework provided implicitly via `Thread.Abort`.
